  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 0
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: '<b>Wait, why is this empty?</b>

    Because it depends on whether you
    are using the new Input System or the old Input.* class.


    <b>NEW INPUT SYSTEM:</b>

    If
    you want to use input binding for the new InputSystem then you first have to
    generate connections for your input actions.

    Here is how:

    1) Open the
    creator window (Window > Settings Creator)

    2) In the Configuration tab open
    the ''Provider Details''.

    3) Drop your InputAction asset into the ''Input
    Action Asset" slot.

    4) Click the ''Create Input Binding Settings'' button.

    5)
    In the hierarchy select the container in the UI you want to add the binding settings
    to (for example ''Settings > ContentCtn > Content (Controls) > Scroll View >
    Viewport > Content > Layout).

    6) Switch to the ''User Interface'' tab and
    click the + button at the bottom. All the input binding settings are prefixed
    with ''InputBinding''. Add those you need.


    <b>OLD Input.* CLASS</b>

    If
    you want to use the old Input class for input then you can add KeyCombination
    settings to your settings list via Tools > Settings Generator > Settings Creator
    Window > "Configuration" Tab > Setings Details > Key Combinations. To add the
    UI check out the "User Interface" tab > Add New Setting.



    HINT: You can
    can also do it all manually by using the "InputKeyConsoleUGUI (Setting)" prefab.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
